1
00:00:05,603 --> 00:00:11,466
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is Madhyaminikaya 38, the Greater Discourse on the Destruction of Craving.

2
00:00:14,808 --> 00:00:16,609
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we'll only go through a bit of it.

3
00:00:17,950 --> 00:00:18,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, fine, yeah.

4
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:22,812
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose the relevant bits, read them out, whatever, and then we'll see what comes out of it.

5
00:00:23,813 --> 00:00:26,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the setting, the beginning of the Sutta.

6
00:00:34,238 --> 00:00:35,199
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on one occasion,

7
00:00:36,422 --> 00:00:45,070
[SPEAKER_00]: A pernicious view or unbeneficial view, because the opposite is from Kalyana view.

8
00:00:45,651 --> 00:00:45,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

9
00:00:45,871 --> 00:00:47,993
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like pernicious as in it's evil.

10
00:00:48,414 --> 00:00:50,135
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like harmful to himself.

11
00:00:50,275 --> 00:00:51,036
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a harmful view.

12
00:00:51,336 --> 00:00:53,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Harmful view to the person who holds it.

13
00:00:53,338 --> 00:00:53,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

14
00:00:54,079 --> 00:01:00,405
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now on that occasion, a harmful view had arisen in a bhikkhu named Sati, son of a fisherman.

15
00:01:02,413 --> 00:01:16,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, as I understand the dhamma taught by the Blessed One, it is in this same consciousness that runs... it is the same consciousness that runs and wanders through the round of rebirths, not another.

16
00:01:16,903 --> 00:01:24,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it is the same consciousness that runs and wanders through saṃsāra, not another consciousness.

17
00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:30,972
[SPEAKER_00]: And several bhikkhus, other bhikkhus... Well, even before we go further,

18
00:01:32,333 --> 00:01:36,014
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, people might just too easily dismiss that.

19
00:01:36,114 --> 00:01:43,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, that's the wrong view, but you want to ask yourself, do you really know why it is wrong here and now?

20
00:01:44,836 --> 00:01:49,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just because the Buddha said so, but do you see for yourself why he said it is wrong view?

21
00:01:52,138 --> 00:01:58,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that would already require somebody to start clarifying the designation consciousness or viññāṇa or whatever you want to call it.

22
00:01:59,876 --> 00:02:10,222
[SPEAKER_01]: And people might realize that very often they end up using these words without any clarity in regard to what they actually mean or what they... What are you designating when you say consciousness?

23
00:02:10,622 --> 00:02:11,823
[SPEAKER_01]: To yourself, not to others.

24
00:02:11,883 --> 00:02:13,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Just sit with yourself and say consciousness, consciousness.

25
00:02:13,684 --> 00:02:14,425
[SPEAKER_01]: What is consciousness?

26
00:02:15,749 --> 00:02:16,931
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you referring to?

27
00:02:16,991 --> 00:02:18,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is that question aimed?

28
00:02:18,152 --> 00:02:18,873
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you want to know?

29
00:02:20,035 --> 00:02:23,379
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you begin this process of clarification, so to speak.

30
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:30,128
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, once you start discerning, and clearly, that sati, the bhikkhu sati, who was

31
00:02:31,092 --> 00:02:33,413
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, people might assume, reading the sutra, he's like an idiot.

32
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:37,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine, the Buddha called him an idiot, but for the different reasons.

33
00:02:37,954 --> 00:02:43,576
[SPEAKER_01]: People might assume, ah, he's just some simpleton, but no, he clearly understood what consciousness is.

34
00:02:44,396 --> 00:02:51,019
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he thought it's that thing that he understood that sort of stays for future lives and so on.

35
00:02:52,259 --> 00:02:55,280
[SPEAKER_01]: And the problem with that, the reason why that view is wrong, is because

36
00:02:56,444 --> 00:03:15,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Any notion of this life and the future life, and if there is a connection between the two, as in, this is what gets reborn, or simply asking a question, what gets reborn, that already assumes that something gets reborn, eh... any of those attitudes require an external point of view to your experience right here, right now.

37
00:03:16,712 --> 00:03:18,153
[SPEAKER_01]: And that in itself, it's atavāda.

38
00:03:19,972 --> 00:03:21,433
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's... he's thinking...

39
00:03:21,453 --> 00:03:33,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because he has a wrong view now, in regard to his own experience, from that, he then assumed further that it is this consciousness that moves around and goes to the next life and next life.

40
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:46,648
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what he said just revealed his wrong view right here, right now, because of which... because of that assumption of atavāda, assumption of the external self, that not just he's not undoing, he's clearly adding more to it.

41
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:52,043
[SPEAKER_01]: With all these other views that he's developing, like, oh, this is the rebirth, and this is that, and the Buddha said this.

42
00:03:52,783 --> 00:03:55,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of all of that, he's actually harming himself.

43
00:03:57,566 --> 00:03:58,187
[SPEAKER_01]: But you cannot...

44
00:03:58,507 --> 00:04:01,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Your only reference point is the experience you have, this life.

45
00:04:01,729 --> 00:04:02,109
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

46
00:04:02,889 --> 00:04:07,112
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you think about before or after this life, it's within this experience as a whole.

47
00:04:07,852 --> 00:04:15,197
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you then, assuming there is this life, and then the next life as the valid next life, perception of it, it's all within this experience, right here, right now.

48
00:04:16,065 --> 00:04:20,506
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that whole thing cannot... well, it certainly cannot be proven in a sense.

49
00:04:20,526 --> 00:04:33,769
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot see it the way you think you're seeing it, which is that external manner from which you can step aside and then see yourself here and then say, yes, when this bit goes, this is the other part that moves to the next life and to the next life.

50
00:04:34,109 --> 00:04:36,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody can see things like that, not even the Buddha himself.

51
00:04:37,270 --> 00:04:44,412
[SPEAKER_01]: So, assuming such a view is entirely, entirely mistaken from the point of view of the nature of things.

52
00:04:48,258 --> 00:04:59,031
[SPEAKER_00]: And what if he's... what if he's... someone says or if he says that it's the same consciousness that is in every experience that I experienced.

53
00:04:59,071 --> 00:05:01,313
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yesterday, it's the same consciousness... Again.

54
00:05:01,333 --> 00:05:02,214
[SPEAKER_00]: ...as it is today.

55
00:05:02,575 --> 00:05:02,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Again.

56
00:05:03,516 --> 00:05:07,901
[SPEAKER_01]: That statement would be rooted in the assumption of the external point of view in regard to the consciousness.

57
00:05:09,413 --> 00:05:13,076
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same consciousness that hears you, the same consciousness that sees you.

58
00:05:13,116 --> 00:05:22,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Some of the interpretations, when they say, like, the moments of consciousness that last a certain number of milliseconds or whatever, like, that's inconceivable.

59
00:05:24,203 --> 00:05:28,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just that you can't measure other people's consciousness, but you can't actually even see your own.

60
00:05:29,506 --> 00:05:33,649
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when people try to say, no, no, no, your samādhi, time stands still, and then you can see, you can't.

61
00:05:34,641 --> 00:05:39,404
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha described what is present in samādhi, it's basically nāmarūpa, viññāṇa, viññāṇa, nāmarūpa.

62
00:05:39,464 --> 00:05:41,445
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the five aggregates that's present in samādhi.

63
00:05:42,205 --> 00:05:46,048
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's samādhi or not samādhi, your experience is there as a whole, the five aggregates.

64
00:05:46,068 --> 00:05:55,033
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you thinking that samādhi gives you some super special power of stopping the time and stepping outside of the five aggregates and then witnessing it with your eyes, that's ridiculous.

65
00:05:55,053 --> 00:05:55,793
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just fantasy.

66
00:05:56,514 --> 00:05:58,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the Buddha's... not just that he said that in samā

67
00:06:02,337 --> 00:06:05,800
[SPEAKER_01]: He said there is still contact, there is still attention, there is still feeling, there is still mindfulness.

68
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:11,604
[SPEAKER_01]: In that one-by-one Majjhima Nikaya about Sariputra's attainments, 111?

69
00:06:13,106 --> 00:06:18,670
[SPEAKER_01]: But then he also said even when people remember previous lives or future lives, they remember one of the aggregates right here, right now.

70
00:06:19,170 --> 00:06:22,233
[SPEAKER_01]: One of the aggregates or all of them within that.

71
00:06:24,014 --> 00:06:25,275
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't step outside.

72
00:06:26,228 --> 00:06:30,652
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, your yesterdays and your tomorrow are within your today.

73
00:06:31,252 --> 00:06:34,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Your previous life, your future life, are also within your today.

74
00:06:34,856 --> 00:06:46,706
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you projecting this linear view of this cosmology, basically, of your rebirth and what moves, it's completely gratuitous and utterly divorced from any kind of, like, reflexive recognition and discernment.

75
00:06:46,746 --> 00:06:49,148
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... well, you can recognize and discern that it's completely wrong.

76
00:06:50,772 --> 00:06:57,257
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody says, oh, my consciousness now and later... Again, they assume external point of view just on a smaller scale.

77
00:06:58,558 --> 00:07:00,419
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about next life, it's about next moment.

78
00:07:00,439 --> 00:07:12,647
[SPEAKER_01]: But still, they're trying to disassociate themselves from their experience as a whole, failing to see that they're entirely enclosed within it, as if you're trapped in a coffin, buried in the ground.

79
00:07:13,288 --> 00:07:14,569
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't conceive the way out.

80
00:07:14,729 --> 00:07:15,590
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even...

81
00:07:15,630 --> 00:07:20,433
[SPEAKER_00]: I was just saying earlier, it's the same consciousness that hears you, it sees you,

82
00:07:21,612 --> 00:07:21,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

83
00:07:22,413 --> 00:07:22,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

84
00:07:22,713 --> 00:07:23,153
[SPEAKER_00]: Right now.

85
00:07:23,413 --> 00:07:23,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

86
00:07:24,614 --> 00:07:24,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

87
00:07:24,894 --> 00:07:26,655
[SPEAKER_00]: That can only be assumed.

88
00:07:26,815 --> 00:07:28,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can say it's a different consciousness.

89
00:07:29,777 --> 00:07:31,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can say it's both same and different.

90
00:07:31,738 --> 00:07:32,579
[SPEAKER_01]: You can mystify it.

91
00:07:33,039 --> 00:07:38,662
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can mystify your mystification and say, well, it's neither the same nor different consciousness.

92
00:07:39,123 --> 00:07:46,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, each of those four statements designates your assumption of external point of view to the consciousness.

93
00:07:47,002 --> 00:07:48,983
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha said, no, I didn't say it is the same.

94
00:07:49,263 --> 00:07:50,464
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't say it isn't the same.

95
00:07:50,544 --> 00:07:52,345
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't say it's neither the same nor not the same.

96
00:07:52,745 --> 00:07:55,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, I didn't say it's both same and not the same, nor neither the same and not the same.

97
00:07:55,927 --> 00:07:59,329
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't say any of it, because saying any of it still feeds your view.

98
00:07:59,489 --> 00:08:00,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Designates your assumption.

99
00:08:00,689 --> 00:08:00,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

100
00:08:01,330 --> 00:08:02,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

101
00:08:02,550 --> 00:08:03,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Designates your assumption.

102
00:08:07,273 --> 00:08:13,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Because consciousness... Consciousness literally means presence of things.

103
00:08:14,076 --> 00:08:14,937
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't even think...

104
00:08:19,065 --> 00:08:26,172
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even think about the pure presence and whatnot that's utterly meaningless and as mystical as it sounds.

105
00:08:26,832 --> 00:08:28,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is presence?

106
00:08:28,094 --> 00:08:29,135
[SPEAKER_01]: How you discern presence?

107
00:08:29,335 --> 00:08:30,636
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of things that are present.

108
00:08:30,836 --> 00:08:32,338
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of phenomena that have arisen.

109
00:08:32,990 --> 00:08:35,131
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the conscious aspect of there is an experience.

110
00:08:35,631 --> 00:08:38,393
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you mystify that presence as some sort of energy.

111
00:08:38,873 --> 00:08:46,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's clearly a thought in your mind, which means that thought is present, which means that thought cannot be that presence which consciousness is.

112
00:08:46,797 --> 00:08:47,597
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just how it works.

113
00:08:47,657 --> 00:08:50,438
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you imagined it, means you're conscious of it.

114
00:08:51,639 --> 00:08:53,320
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't be conscious of your consciousness.

115
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:54,380
[SPEAKER_00]: And what's that saying?

116
00:08:55,261 --> 00:08:59,803
[SPEAKER_00]: That's saying that wisdom is to be developed... Consciousness to be understood.

117
00:09:01,232 --> 00:09:01,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Understood.

118
00:09:02,552 --> 00:09:03,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Not seen directly.

119
00:09:04,373 --> 00:09:04,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

120
00:09:04,733 --> 00:09:05,153
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

121
00:09:05,333 --> 00:09:06,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that would require you to step outside.

122
00:09:07,453 --> 00:09:08,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

123
00:09:08,474 --> 00:09:09,514
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a good point.

124
00:09:10,614 --> 00:09:11,454
[SPEAKER_01]: You can discern it.

125
00:09:11,574 --> 00:09:12,475
[SPEAKER_01]: You can understand it.

126
00:09:14,055 --> 00:09:15,515
[SPEAKER_01]: And by doing so, you develop wisdom.

127
00:09:16,495 --> 00:09:27,278
[SPEAKER_01]: By understanding the consciousness, you understand the nature of presence, the nature of arising, the nature of manifestation.

128
00:09:27,358 --> 00:09:28,098
[SPEAKER_00]: And where you are.

129
00:09:28,795 --> 00:09:29,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

130
00:09:29,575 --> 00:09:30,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yes.

131
00:09:30,115 --> 00:09:33,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there won't be much left of you if you understand things correctly in that sense.

132
00:09:34,816 --> 00:09:39,157
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you maintain your sense of self through that external assumption to your own experience.

133
00:09:39,577 --> 00:09:40,378
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just how it works.

134
00:09:40,458 --> 00:09:41,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the other.

135
00:09:44,819 --> 00:09:47,079
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, it's very... it's as subtle as that.

136
00:09:47,139 --> 00:09:48,979
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like a putuja who goes,

137
00:09:52,538 --> 00:09:57,962
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like Puthujana goes around saying, oh, I think I am this, or I am that.

138
00:09:58,142 --> 00:09:58,343
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

139
00:09:58,723 --> 00:10:08,771
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a subtle assumption that you can see yourself in the external manner, for example, or that you can see what's in for you, or you can see the next day in a manner that's independent of you.

140
00:10:09,111 --> 00:10:10,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumption of a public world.

141
00:10:11,433 --> 00:10:17,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumption of a public space and time that we are in, not that it's in our experience, and so on.

142
00:10:18,495 --> 00:10:24,359
[SPEAKER_01]: All those things go back to that subtle factor of ātavāda.

143
00:10:25,039 --> 00:10:27,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumption of the external point of view, one way or the other.

144
00:10:30,343 --> 00:10:34,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Subtle assumption of the external view.

145
00:10:34,866 --> 00:10:40,430
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll continue.

146
00:10:40,730 --> 00:10:43,452
[SPEAKER_00]: The subtle assumption of an external view.

147
00:10:43,912 --> 00:10:45,313
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to your own experience.

148
00:10:45,633 --> 00:10:46,694
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to your own experience.

149
00:10:46,974 --> 00:10:47,995
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to your own self.

150
00:10:50,886 --> 00:10:52,587
[SPEAKER_00]: That is why he... why he's wrong.

151
00:10:52,627 --> 00:10:53,747
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's a wrong view.

152
00:10:53,888 --> 00:10:54,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

153
00:10:56,029 --> 00:11:08,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So, several bhikkhus, having heard about this sati... bhikkhu sati, went to him and asked him, friend sati, is it true that such a unhelpful, harmful view...

154
00:11:08,577 --> 00:11:09,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Harmful view.

155
00:11:09,258 --> 00:11:10,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Harmful view has arisen in you?

156
00:11:20,825 --> 00:11:23,227
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he says, exactly so, friends.

157
00:11:23,467 --> 00:11:31,352
[SPEAKER_00]: As I understand the dhamma taught by the Blessed One, it is the same consciousness that runs and wanders through the round of rebirths, not another.

158
00:11:32,213 --> 00:11:42,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, those bhikkhus desiring to detach him from that harmful view, pressed and questioned, cross-questioned him, said thus, friends sati, do not say so.

159
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:45,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Do not misrepresent the Blessed One.

160
00:11:45,242 --> 00:11:47,323
[SPEAKER_00]: It is not good to misrepresent the Blessed One.

161
00:11:48,137 --> 00:11:59,961
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, there is no presence without something being present.

162
00:12:00,542 --> 00:12:03,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Since without a condition, there is no origination of consciousness.

163
00:12:03,523 --> 00:12:04,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there you go.

164
00:12:05,463 --> 00:12:10,065
[SPEAKER_01]: But that translation, though, origination could imply the time.

165
00:12:11,159 --> 00:12:14,281
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, the first thing comes, then the consciousness arises a second.

166
00:12:14,301 --> 00:12:15,121
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not the case.

167
00:12:15,201 --> 00:12:22,886
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, consciousness and things that you are conscious of, as in nāmarūpa, the all, are like two sticks simultaneously propping each other up.

168
00:12:23,586 --> 00:12:26,948
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove one, the other one cannot remain standing.

169
00:12:28,208 --> 00:12:36,393
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not a momentary thing that you can observe, because if you think you're observing consciousness and nāmarūpa's dependence, means you're assuming you're outside of it.

170
00:12:38,484 --> 00:12:45,147
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this particular case, even in the broadest terms, you can't have presence unless there's something present.

171
00:12:45,807 --> 00:12:52,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even when you think the word, letters, presence in your mind, that is present for you.

172
00:12:53,571 --> 00:12:54,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts are present.

173
00:12:54,912 --> 00:12:58,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagination, abstraction, it is present, means you are conscious of it.

174
00:12:59,354 --> 00:13:01,575
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you can know things at any given time.

175
00:13:03,536 --> 00:13:04,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're present.

176
00:13:06,679 --> 00:13:27,758
[SPEAKER_01]: But people, sort of, would attach presence... sorry, would detach presence from coarse things, and then, when they think about the presence, the pure presence, the presence of the presence, the presence of your awareness, the... all of these things, it's... it's... they fail to see that that also isn't a reason thing, that it's present for them, and thus cannot stand for that presence of that very thing.

177
00:13:29,539 --> 00:13:33,563
[SPEAKER_01]: And he clearly assumes that that presence is independent and it moves around.

178
00:13:35,252 --> 00:13:36,753
[SPEAKER_01]: for the next life and next life.

179
00:13:37,113 --> 00:13:42,335
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha said... they said to him... didn't the Buddha say that the consciousness cannot stand on its own?

180
00:13:42,755 --> 00:13:43,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

181
00:13:43,636 --> 00:13:45,837
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not a question if it's the same consciousness or different.

182
00:13:45,857 --> 00:13:52,900
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is consciousness of any kind is inconceivable without something to be conscious of simultaneously.

183
00:13:54,741 --> 00:13:58,623
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't step outside of it to see it because you are it.

184
00:13:59,636 --> 00:14:02,817
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Nyanavira said in one of his letters, maybe.

185
00:14:03,177 --> 00:14:10,660
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, if you attend to nāmarūpa, well, your experience is undermined by the independence of viññāṇa.

186
00:14:11,161 --> 00:14:19,684
[SPEAKER_01]: If you try to attend to the presence, the nature of viññāṇa, your experience is undermined by the presence of... independence of nāmarūpa.

187
00:14:20,284 --> 00:14:27,587
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you can't take two things to the same extent at the same time from... because that would require you to step outside of them, but you can't because you are them.

188
00:14:28,776 --> 00:14:33,118
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I'm talking about the peripheral, discerning the background of whatever you're attending, whatever is present.

189
00:14:36,039 --> 00:14:37,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyhow, go.

190
00:14:38,880 --> 00:14:40,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, no pure consciousness.

191
00:14:40,861 --> 00:14:42,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

192
00:14:42,042 --> 00:14:44,803
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a complete fallacy, the idea of pure consciousness.

193
00:14:44,983 --> 00:14:46,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's a concept.

194
00:14:46,524 --> 00:14:51,506
[SPEAKER_01]: That's an idea that is present as such, and people are obviously not conscious of it, because they think that's what consciousness is.

195
00:14:56,330 --> 00:14:59,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you say presence or presence, one of those presences is not presence.

196
00:15:00,771 --> 00:15:02,291
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the object of the other presence.

197
00:15:03,011 --> 00:15:06,692
[SPEAKER_01]: But you trying to level them on the same ground, means not understanding either.

198
00:15:07,312 --> 00:15:08,793
Yeah.

199
00:15:10,133 --> 00:15:11,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it an assumption?

200
00:15:12,973 --> 00:15:13,714
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, of course.

201
00:15:13,734 --> 00:15:14,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Pure consciousness, isn't it?

202
00:15:14,534 --> 00:15:15,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it is an assumption.

203
00:15:15,314 --> 00:15:17,314
[SPEAKER_00]: So, together with... Yeah, yeah, yeah.

204
00:15:17,374 --> 00:15:18,014
[SPEAKER_00]: It's wrong, it's wrong.

205
00:15:18,114 --> 00:15:22,855
[SPEAKER_01]: And you maintain assumption not because you're actively choosing to hold on to it.

206
00:15:22,875 --> 00:15:25,556
[SPEAKER_01]: You're maintaining it by not undoing it.

207
00:15:26,205 --> 00:15:27,385
[SPEAKER_01]: By not fully understanding it.

208
00:15:27,605 --> 00:15:29,486
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all it takes to maintain ignorance.

209
00:15:29,526 --> 00:15:33,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Not make the effort to understand the ignorance.

210
00:15:33,267 --> 00:15:36,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people think attachments and assumptions and so on is something you do.

211
00:15:37,148 --> 00:15:39,849
[SPEAKER_01]: And then all you have to do is stop doing it and then you're free from it.

212
00:15:40,830 --> 00:15:42,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm attached to my possessions.

213
00:15:42,190 --> 00:15:43,550
[SPEAKER_01]: I will throw away my possessions.

214
00:15:43,971 --> 00:15:45,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I'm free from attachment.

215
00:15:45,251 --> 00:15:45,431
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

216
00:15:47,812 --> 00:15:49,572
[SPEAKER_01]: I assume the external point of view.

217
00:15:50,052 --> 00:15:52,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so then I'll just sort of stop assuming it.

218
00:15:53,734 --> 00:15:54,974
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just stop assuming it.

219
00:15:56,023 --> 00:16:08,307
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to stop assuming the external view is to understand the mistake of external view, the incompatibility, the discrepancy, the impossible, inconceivable aspect of it.

220
00:16:08,847 --> 00:16:16,909
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand that the truly external point of view, which you seem to imply with your assumption, is inconceivable, then the assumption becomes redundant and it fades.

221
00:16:20,130 --> 00:16:23,191
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is that pañcupādānakkhandhā?

222
00:16:23,611 --> 00:16:23,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Uh-huh.

223
00:16:25,426 --> 00:16:33,972
[SPEAKER_00]: The five aggregates affected by assumption is basically five aggregates that you think you are external to.

224
00:16:33,992 --> 00:16:34,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

225
00:16:34,172 --> 00:16:36,934
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep saying, that is the starting point for everyone.

226
00:16:37,394 --> 00:16:40,957
[SPEAKER_01]: People who really think, okay, so there is this five aggregates that I keep taking.

227
00:16:41,117 --> 00:16:41,617
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

228
00:16:41,857 --> 00:16:46,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Putujana, for him, the threshold is pañca, upādāna, khandhā.

229
00:16:46,340 --> 00:16:47,481
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the threshold for him.

230
00:16:48,061 --> 00:16:49,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Five assumed aggregates.

231
00:16:51,164 --> 00:16:54,706
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he can't see five aggregates by themselves, because if he starts seeing that,

232
00:16:55,322 --> 00:17:00,623
[SPEAKER_01]: He would start seeing the independence of them, he would start to lose his own atavāda, he would stop being a pūtujana.

233
00:17:04,264 --> 00:17:19,606
[SPEAKER_01]: But if he thinks he sees them, as in, oh, I'm seeing the fagagrits and now I'm practicing not attachment to them, that's the practice that leads me to sotāpati and whatnot, he won't make the effort to really see them, because he thinks he already sees them.

234
00:17:21,546 --> 00:17:24,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so that's what you're saying is, where you start,

235
00:17:24,828 --> 00:17:25,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Is ignorance?

236
00:17:26,069 --> 00:17:26,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

237
00:17:26,709 --> 00:17:27,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Is in that assumption?

238
00:17:27,790 --> 00:17:27,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

239
00:17:28,770 --> 00:17:32,472
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one should be clear on that.

240
00:17:32,492 --> 00:17:33,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, absolutely.

241
00:17:33,973 --> 00:17:41,077
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you recognize that you're ignorant, it means anything you think you know, you must not take for granted.

242
00:17:42,578 --> 00:17:49,362
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you must investigate, you must not leave anything unturned, you must look for the meaning, you must clarify the meaning and so on.

243
00:17:49,382 --> 00:17:51,163
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like all the Buddha was talking about.

244
00:17:52,491 --> 00:17:56,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise, you will assume things that you think you know.

245
00:17:58,034 --> 00:18:00,736
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, you won't really get to know what they are.

246
00:18:06,179 --> 00:18:06,759
[SPEAKER_00]: So, continue.

247
00:18:07,220 --> 00:18:07,740
[SPEAKER_00]: The sutta.

248
00:18:09,121 --> 00:18:09,981
[SPEAKER_00]: And they were questioning him.

249
00:18:10,602 --> 00:18:12,343
[SPEAKER_00]: The sati, bhikkhu sati, the other bhikkhus.

250
00:18:13,149 --> 00:18:27,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, although pressed and questioned and cross-questioned by those bhikkhus in this way, the bhikkhu sati, son of a fisherman, still adhered, didn't want to move from his view, adhered to that harmful view and continued to insist upon it.

251
00:18:28,767 --> 00:18:32,991
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, he clearly did not see their view as a harmful view.

252
00:18:34,148 --> 00:18:41,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when the view is designated, harmful view, that's designated by the one who is reciting the sutta for the audience, saying, oh, he holds a harmful view.

253
00:18:41,873 --> 00:18:47,397
[SPEAKER_01]: But, from the point of view of sati, the bhikkhu sati, he clearly thought that's what the Buddha was teaching.

254
00:18:47,497 --> 00:18:50,879
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he thought the other monks, they were trying to dissuade him are wrong.

255
00:18:51,059 --> 00:18:52,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he thought they were wrong.

256
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:55,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he thought, exactly, he thought, no, this is the right view, you have the wrong view.

257
00:18:55,962 --> 00:18:56,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

258
00:18:57,843 --> 00:19:01,886
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, the other bhikkhus, they went, they couldn't do anything about it.

259
00:19:01,926 --> 00:19:02,046
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

260
00:19:04,446 --> 00:19:08,469
[SPEAKER_00]: I told the Buddha about this and asked for his help.

261
00:19:10,270 --> 00:19:18,415
[SPEAKER_00]: And he... so they... the Buddha asked for them to call him Sati, Bhikkhu Sati, so he could question it.

262
00:19:37,055 --> 00:19:47,020
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anyway, Bhikkhu Sati comes to the Buddha, and the Buddha asks him, Sati, is it true that the following harmful view has arisen in you?

263
00:19:47,980 --> 00:19:54,443
[SPEAKER_00]: As I understand the Dhamma taught by the Blessed One, it is the same consciousness that runs and wanders through the round of rebirths, not another.

264
00:19:55,924 --> 00:19:58,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Sati replies, exactly so, Venerable Sir.

265
00:19:58,985 --> 00:20:00,246
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly how I understand it.

266
00:20:00,826 --> 00:20:04,668
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha asks him, what is that consciousness, Sati?

267
00:20:08,238 --> 00:20:21,841
[SPEAKER_00]: That's actually quite a contemporary view of consciousness among many people.

268
00:20:22,821 --> 00:20:30,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people think that it's your consciousness that does all this, that gets the retribution of karma, that gets rebirth and so on.

269
00:20:30,982 --> 00:20:33,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if they don't necessarily call it the consciousness,

270
00:20:35,301 --> 00:20:36,381
[SPEAKER_01]: You might use a different word.

271
00:20:36,401 --> 00:20:38,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't use any words at all, but a view is still there.

272
00:20:42,363 --> 00:20:47,264
[SPEAKER_00]: That which speaks and feels and experiences, here and now.

273
00:20:47,284 --> 00:20:56,027
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that you even refer to it as that, means you have assumed it, as if you can see it as an object.

274
00:20:56,067 --> 00:20:58,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's that which does all these things.

275
00:20:59,228 --> 00:21:03,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that that thing that you're designating is that which does all these things, is a present thing.

276
00:21:04,869 --> 00:21:07,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means there is a presence in regard to that thing.

277
00:21:07,671 --> 00:21:11,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means that's what consciousness is, that presence, not what you are designating it to be.

278
00:21:24,039 --> 00:21:28,782
[SPEAKER_00]: And so the Buddha replies, misguided man,

279
00:21:30,962 --> 00:21:32,823
[SPEAKER_01]: I think he was a bit harsher than that.

280
00:21:33,164 --> 00:21:42,791
[SPEAKER_00]: In the Pali, it said, he calls Bhikkhu Sati, a morga purisa, which translates as foolish man, but idiot.

281
00:21:43,311 --> 00:21:43,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Useless idiot.

282
00:21:44,072 --> 00:21:45,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Useless, stupid man.

283
00:21:46,214 --> 00:21:47,114
[SPEAKER_00]: So, an idiot.

284
00:21:48,315 --> 00:21:56,021
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, you stupid man, to whom have you ever known me to teach the dhamma in that way?

285
00:21:58,720 --> 00:22:09,436
[SPEAKER_00]: You stupid man, have I not stated in many ways consciousness to be dependently arisen, since without a condition there is no origination of consciousness.

286
00:22:10,598 --> 00:22:12,100
[SPEAKER_00]: But you, stupid man,

287
00:22:14,974 --> 00:22:24,841
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why is that?

288
00:22:26,583 --> 00:22:27,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because...

289
00:22:27,503 --> 00:22:28,484
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, just because.

290
00:22:29,305 --> 00:22:33,428
[SPEAKER_01]: In modern terms, you say, or just because he misunderstood what consciousness is.

291
00:22:34,108 --> 00:22:41,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, the Buddha publicly, in front of the whole assembly, keeps calling that man, stupid man, an idiot, a useless man, a fool.

292
00:22:42,727 --> 00:22:44,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he misunderstood.

293
00:22:45,168 --> 00:22:48,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because he'd done anything wrong, by body, speech or mind, so to speak.

294
00:22:49,271 --> 00:22:52,633
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, he hasn't, like, broken the rules or anything like that.

295
00:22:52,713 --> 00:22:54,954
[SPEAKER_01]: He just misunderstood what the Buddha was teaching.

296
00:22:55,654 --> 00:23:02,738
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how serious that is, that the Buddha says, you've not just injured yourself, but, like, lots of future demerits, so to speak.

297
00:23:03,098 --> 00:23:05,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he's been probably going around saying that that's what the Buddha is teaching.

298
00:23:07,041 --> 00:23:12,426
[SPEAKER_01]: He was teaching that which he thought the Buddha was teaching, while that's not what the dhamma is.

299
00:23:12,806 --> 00:23:12,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

300
00:23:13,707 --> 00:23:14,427
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all it takes.

301
00:23:16,769 --> 00:23:17,390
Yeah.

302
00:23:19,872 --> 00:23:28,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Not clearly knowing that you know, but basing your... your sort of... how to put it?

303
00:23:31,302 --> 00:23:33,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, like, founding your understanding.

304
00:23:34,728 --> 00:23:53,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Establishing your understanding upon, like, lack of clarity, upon something that's just agreeable, or pleasing to you, or it makes sense, without really understanding why it makes sense, to what extent it makes sense, and how it makes sense, and why, without really understanding things that don't make sense, why they don't make sense, why are they wrong, why are the right things right.

305
00:23:53,901 --> 00:23:58,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, without understanding right is right, wrong is wrong, which is a criteria for a sotāpanna.

306
00:24:00,685 --> 00:24:01,586
[SPEAKER_01]: And everybody knows that.

307
00:24:01,606 --> 00:24:02,266
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's the thing.

308
00:24:04,142 --> 00:24:06,064
[SPEAKER_01]: He might have... sure, you might feel sorry for him.

309
00:24:06,104 --> 00:24:09,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, well, he only misunderstood what consciousness is.

310
00:24:09,326 --> 00:24:12,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Why was the Buddha so harsh, publicly humiliating him?

311
00:24:12,489 --> 00:24:16,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he go... you know, everybody would be humiliated.

312
00:24:16,832 --> 00:24:26,060
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, that's not... he's responsible for it, because he wouldn't have arrived at it without willfully ignoring the fact that he already knows

313
00:24:27,433 --> 00:24:34,495
[SPEAKER_01]: That he hasn't fully penetrated and understood and freed himself from suffering or understood wholesomeness, wholesomeness, unwholesomeness, all of those things.

314
00:24:35,956 --> 00:24:39,217
[SPEAKER_01]: He basically, through his carelessness, ignored that aspect.

315
00:24:39,557 --> 00:24:43,238
[SPEAKER_01]: They would have made him concerned before he started speaking about what the Buddha is teaching.

316
00:24:43,438 --> 00:24:45,719
[SPEAKER_01]: He just wouldn't be doing it unless he's clear.

317
00:24:46,259 --> 00:24:50,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, certainly, if he's doing it, he would make sure that he's not sure.

318
00:24:50,980 --> 00:24:56,522
[SPEAKER_01]: He would make sure to tell people that he's not sure, but then you wouldn't... why would you... you wouldn't be teaching them, wouldn't you?

319
00:24:58,031 --> 00:25:06,694
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the fact that he was going around and saying this is what the Lord teaches, coming out of his own lack of effort to understand, yeah, that's why it's so bad.

320
00:25:06,954 --> 00:25:09,134
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he's so responsible for it.

321
00:25:09,755 --> 00:25:13,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Although, like, yeah, in today's modern terms, you would think, well, what's the big deal?

322
00:25:13,696 --> 00:25:15,556
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, many people misunderstand many things.

323
00:25:15,857 --> 00:25:16,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

324
00:25:16,477 --> 00:25:17,397
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how big a deal it is.

325
00:25:24,058 --> 00:25:25,139
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha goes on.

326
00:25:25,539 --> 00:25:29,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus, the other bhikkhus over there with us.

327
00:25:30,322 --> 00:25:31,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhus, what do you think?

328
00:25:32,763 --> 00:25:40,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Has this bhikkhu Sati, son of a fisherman, kindled even a spark of wisdom in this dhamma and discipline?

329
00:25:40,768 --> 00:25:42,108
[SPEAKER_01]: While bhikkhu Sati is sitting next to him?

330
00:25:42,249 --> 00:25:42,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

331
00:25:43,029 --> 00:25:43,249
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

332
00:25:44,330 --> 00:25:46,471
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like public humiliation in a school class.

333
00:25:47,171 --> 00:25:47,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically.

334
00:25:48,192 --> 00:25:51,254
[SPEAKER_00]: As everyone, all the other bhikkhus say, how could he, Venerable Sir?

335
00:25:52,114 --> 00:25:52,915
[SPEAKER_00]: No, Venerable Sir.

336
00:25:54,238 --> 00:26:05,586
[SPEAKER_00]: When this was said, the Bhikkhu Sati, son of a fisherman, sat silent, dismayed, embarrassed, with shoulders drooping, depressed, head down, glum, without response.

337
00:26:06,627 --> 00:26:10,369
[SPEAKER_00]: So he wasn't... he wasn't very happy, one could say.

338
00:26:12,131 --> 00:26:15,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, knowing this, the Blessed One told him,

339
00:26:18,103 --> 00:26:19,364
[SPEAKER_00]: You stupid man!

340
00:26:19,884 --> 00:26:20,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, reiterating it.

341
00:26:21,044 --> 00:26:24,326
[SPEAKER_00]: You will be recognized by your own harmful view.

342
00:26:25,226 --> 00:26:26,547
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what people will remember you for.

343
00:26:26,907 --> 00:26:27,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

344
00:26:28,187 --> 00:26:28,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

345
00:26:28,688 --> 00:26:31,429
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, for generations... Yeah.

346
00:26:31,569 --> 00:26:36,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Two and a half millennia later, still, his name is still associated with it.

347
00:26:36,631 --> 00:26:36,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

348
00:26:37,411 --> 00:26:41,033
[SPEAKER_00]: So, while he's sitting there, he says, I shall question the bhikkhus on this matter.

349
00:26:42,379 --> 00:26:50,443
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, just to point out there, like, you know, some people might think, like, well, how... why would the Buddha do that?

350
00:26:52,204 --> 00:26:54,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course, the Buddha is right, but why was he so rude?

351
00:26:55,106 --> 00:26:56,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he wasn't.

352
00:26:57,607 --> 00:27:03,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the Buddha has... well, not even the Buddha... like, even Sotāpanna would have that perspective already, let alone an Arahant.

353
00:27:04,020 --> 00:27:05,941
[SPEAKER_01]: You really know important from unimportant.

354
00:27:06,361 --> 00:27:14,126
[SPEAKER_01]: So, upsetting somebody here and now, making him feel ashamed of his wrong view, boo-hoo, basically, he's going to be upset.

355
00:27:14,526 --> 00:27:21,030
[SPEAKER_01]: But, at least, he might shake into his core and he'll make the effort and understand, as it often happened, when they felt ashamed.

356
00:27:21,090 --> 00:27:26,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And even the... there's that... the... the... the chanting that's kind of done in the temples in the morning.

357
00:27:26,734 --> 00:27:30,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, will I be ashamed at the end of my life if I'm questioned by other monks?

358
00:27:31,177 --> 00:27:32,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, where is my understanding?

359
00:27:32,497 --> 00:27:33,338
[SPEAKER_01]: What did I develop?

360
00:27:35,176 --> 00:27:38,459
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a valid thing that can spur you to make the effort rightly.

361
00:27:39,660 --> 00:27:44,325
[SPEAKER_01]: And, again, the Buddha's perspective was not just, oh, I must not upset anyone in this life.

362
00:27:44,966 --> 00:27:45,086
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

363
00:27:45,166 --> 00:27:55,317
[SPEAKER_01]: He could see that because of that wrong view, if that bhikkhu doesn't abandon it, not just that he'll remain a bhutujjana, that he won't become an arahant, he's gonna end up in some horrible rebirth,

364
00:27:56,353 --> 00:28:04,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he's been going around saying, misrepresenting the Buddha, that then resulted in other people also thinking the Buddha was teaching that, not what the real dhamma is, and so on.

365
00:28:04,460 --> 00:28:15,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, being responsible, directly or indirectly, for many people's misunderstandings, that then resulted in their misdeeds and everything else, would be on him and his harmful view.

366
00:28:16,570 --> 00:28:18,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha could see that unless he abandons it,

367
00:28:19,974 --> 00:28:24,356
[SPEAKER_01]: He's going to pay the price that really nobody wants to be paying.

368
00:28:25,056 --> 00:28:31,779
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is, from that point of view, upsetting him now, for a few days, that's nothing in comparison.

369
00:28:32,160 --> 00:28:39,063
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha meant when he said, like, when he gave that simile, like, what if you have, like, a single child and if it swallows something,

370
00:28:40,048 --> 00:28:45,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Wouldn't you stick your arm in its throat and pull that thing out, even if you make the child bleed and hurt it?

371
00:28:45,332 --> 00:28:47,233
[SPEAKER_01]: You would, because it would at least save its life.

372
00:28:47,533 --> 00:28:53,237
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, in the long run, you would save it by harming it initially, by upsetting it initially.

373
00:28:53,597 --> 00:28:54,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

374
00:28:55,939 --> 00:29:04,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, in a way, because it's been recorded, sati... well, who knows if he changed his view, but...

375
00:29:04,084 --> 00:29:04,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

376
00:29:05,965 --> 00:29:08,427
[SPEAKER_00]: This whole incident can help others to...

377
00:29:09,489 --> 00:29:10,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, clearly, yeah.

378
00:29:10,690 --> 00:29:20,634
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that it's there as a teaching example means sati... but the fact that he was able to sit through that means he did sort of do some good.

379
00:29:22,635 --> 00:29:32,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha continues to question the other bhikkhus and they are all in agreement with him about sati.

380
00:29:33,821 --> 00:29:36,262
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, then he makes... the Buddha makes a statement about consciousness

381
00:29:38,119 --> 00:29:43,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Because consciousness is reckoned by the particular condition dependent upon which it arises.

382
00:29:44,741 --> 00:29:49,003
[SPEAKER_00]: When consciousness arises depending on the I and forms, it is reckoned as I-consciousness.

383
00:29:49,603 --> 00:29:49,923
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on.

384
00:29:50,363 --> 00:29:55,705
[SPEAKER_01]: If the sight is present, you're talking about presence of sights.

385
00:29:57,026 --> 00:30:03,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Sound, smell, taste, touches are present, you're talking about sound, smell, taste, touches, their presence.

386
00:30:05,322 --> 00:30:08,205
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the presence of these things is what consciousness is.

387
00:30:08,845 --> 00:30:14,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, trying to even... even reasoning consciousness as a separate entity, is completely mistaken.

388
00:30:17,152 --> 00:30:23,718
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the right, the correct reasoning is the reasoning that doesn't disassociate consciousness from the things that it's inseparable from.

389
00:30:24,018 --> 00:30:30,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, even when you think the world consciousness in itself, that thing is present in your mind that you are conscious of.

390
00:30:31,636 --> 00:30:38,261
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you have to do is stop misconceiving that that word, that term, stands for that which presence of things is.

391
00:30:39,122 --> 00:30:40,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't have pure presence.

392
00:30:40,603 --> 00:30:42,685
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't go below it and see it in front of you.

393
00:30:44,667 --> 00:30:46,928
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always behind, so to speak.

394
00:30:48,329 --> 00:30:51,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, yeah, presence of this is different than presence of this.

395
00:30:52,012 --> 00:30:55,275
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, overall, you can say presence is there because there is something present.

396
00:30:55,415 --> 00:30:56,376
[SPEAKER_01]: There is this present.

397
00:30:57,381 --> 00:30:58,501
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

398
00:30:58,842 --> 00:31:01,363
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the most basic definition of patitsaṃpāda.

399
00:31:04,164 --> 00:31:04,944
[SPEAKER_00]: It gives us samādhi.

400
00:31:05,404 --> 00:31:09,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Just as a fire is reckoned by the particular condition dependent on which it burns.

401
00:31:09,986 --> 00:31:12,828
[SPEAKER_00]: When fire burns dependent on logs, it is reckoned as a log fire.

402
00:31:13,928 --> 00:31:18,610
[SPEAKER_00]: If it burns dependent on sticks, it's reckoned as a stick fire.

403
00:31:19,510 --> 00:31:20,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's exactly what I'm saying here.

404
00:31:23,574 --> 00:31:24,114
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not the same...

405
00:31:24,134 --> 00:31:26,316
[SPEAKER_01]: If the sights are present, then it's presence of sights.

406
00:31:26,336 --> 00:31:26,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

407
00:31:26,776 --> 00:31:27,717
[SPEAKER_01]: The sounds are present.

408
00:31:28,297 --> 00:31:39,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, but you can, as the nature of the aggregate is, if you want to discern it, you see, it doesn't matter what presence... it doesn't matter that what presence as presence of what is present.

409
00:31:39,425 --> 00:31:40,066
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

410
00:31:40,446 --> 00:31:44,309
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is that there is a presence there in itself of any kind.

411
00:31:45,189 --> 00:31:48,792
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, thoughts, doesn't matter.

412
00:31:48,932 --> 00:31:49,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence is there.

413
00:31:51,327 --> 00:31:53,288
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what feeling is there.

414
00:31:53,668 --> 00:31:56,229
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what intentions are there, and so on.

415
00:31:59,011 --> 00:32:01,932
[SPEAKER_01]: That's if you try to understand it from the point of view of the aggregates.

416
00:32:02,953 --> 00:32:14,138
[SPEAKER_01]: When you stop being concerned even with that relatively non-particular designation of presence of sights, which is quite a general designation already.

417
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:20,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the presence of this particular sight on account of that particular sight.

418
00:32:21,582 --> 00:32:23,823
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's a sight, there is a presence of sights.

419
00:32:25,243 --> 00:32:28,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Means there is the eye consciousness, as the suttas would call it.

420
00:32:29,825 --> 00:32:38,489
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that, you can go even more broadly, more peripherally, and that's how you start discerning the aggregates, when you say, well, it doesn't matter even what sense.

421
00:32:39,049 --> 00:32:42,991
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter what sense the consciousness, sort of, is presence of.

422
00:32:44,311 --> 00:32:45,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Consciousness is there.

423
00:32:45,272 --> 00:32:46,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence is there.

424
00:32:46,990 --> 00:32:52,034
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why consciousness is the last aggregate you can discern in such manner, after you cover everything else.

425
00:32:52,474 --> 00:32:54,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everything else appears, to some extent.

426
00:32:55,337 --> 00:32:57,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Means everything else you are conscious of.

427
00:32:57,979 --> 00:32:59,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else is present.

428
00:33:00,621 --> 00:33:07,747
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you define, fully clarified, everything that is present, then you can understand the nature of presence,

429
00:33:08,418 --> 00:33:13,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Without making it a foreground of a new present object, which is what people do.

430
00:33:13,743 --> 00:33:17,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, yoniso manasikāra and peripheral awareness and everything else we've been talking about.

431
00:33:19,889 --> 00:33:20,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyway, go ahead.

432
00:33:22,631 --> 00:33:26,735
[SPEAKER_00]: And he continues to question the bhikkhus on...

433
00:33:26,755 --> 00:33:27,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, well...

434
00:33:27,896 --> 00:33:31,900
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, bhikkhus, do you see this has come to be?

435
00:33:33,021 --> 00:33:33,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah!

436
00:33:34,128 --> 00:33:34,828
[SPEAKER_01]: I know that bit.

437
00:33:35,648 --> 00:33:42,750
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually, the Pali says, monks, do you see this is or this has arisen?

438
00:33:42,770 --> 00:33:52,013
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't say this has come to be, which implies, again, origination, approaches that you observe of something coming into being.

439
00:33:52,093 --> 00:33:52,273
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

440
00:33:52,833 --> 00:33:57,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Monks, do you see this is present or this is?

441
00:33:57,335 --> 00:33:58,455
[SPEAKER_01]: That's actually what the Pali says.

442
00:33:59,786 --> 00:34:03,208
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember I translated that bit a long time ago in one of the essays.

443
00:34:03,328 --> 00:34:04,109
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't remember which one.

444
00:34:04,449 --> 00:34:06,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe I never even published it.

445
00:34:08,631 --> 00:34:08,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

446
00:34:09,151 --> 00:34:10,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Monks, do you see this is?

447
00:34:16,796 --> 00:34:17,676
[SPEAKER_01]: Or this is present.

448
00:34:17,716 --> 00:34:19,097
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a very present tense, basically.

449
00:34:19,117 --> 00:34:21,118
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like this has come to be.

450
00:34:22,879 --> 00:34:26,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, I saw this, having been before, has come to be now.

451
00:34:27,442 --> 00:34:29,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see this is?

452
00:34:29,570 --> 00:34:29,770
[SPEAKER_01]: This.

453
00:34:30,310 --> 00:34:30,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see this?

454
00:34:31,771 --> 00:34:33,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Monks, do you see this is present?

455
00:34:33,492 --> 00:34:34,212
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say like that.

456
00:34:34,232 --> 00:34:36,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because there's bhūta.

457
00:34:36,873 --> 00:34:37,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhūta-vidhānti.

458
00:34:38,074 --> 00:34:38,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, this.

459
00:34:39,874 --> 00:34:40,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see this?

460
00:34:40,955 --> 00:34:41,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see this?

461
00:34:41,795 --> 00:34:42,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Monks, do you see this?

462
00:34:42,916 --> 00:34:43,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, this.

463
00:34:44,556 --> 00:34:45,097
[SPEAKER_00]: And I say, yes.

464
00:34:45,837 --> 00:34:47,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we see that this is present.

465
00:34:48,578 --> 00:34:54,220
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because, do you see, its origination occurs with that as nutriment?

466
00:34:54,581 --> 00:34:55,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Hmm.

467
00:34:55,941 --> 00:34:57,902
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had to, I had to punch it, wasn't it?

468
00:35:00,390 --> 00:35:04,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see that it originated with that as fuel?

469
00:35:07,474 --> 00:35:10,475
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's the sights you're talking about, take a sight.

470
00:35:13,537 --> 00:35:18,220
[SPEAKER_01]: We look at the same sight and you say, do you see that these mountains around us are present?

471
00:35:18,500 --> 00:35:18,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see them?

472
00:35:19,500 --> 00:35:19,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

473
00:35:21,601 --> 00:35:22,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, would you be able to see them?

474
00:35:24,963 --> 00:35:28,285
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, would you be able... would they be able to be present for you?

475
00:35:30,158 --> 00:35:47,924
[SPEAKER_01]: If there's nothing there to be present, if there is no eye to see, if there are no forms to be seen, if the eye doesn't operate well, if the eye organ is impaired, if something happens to the forms, if any of those things are utterly out of your control, if they were not there, would you be still able to say, this is present?

476
00:35:48,364 --> 00:35:50,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be able to think about it as present?

477
00:35:50,305 --> 00:35:50,745
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't.

478
00:35:53,126 --> 00:35:55,467
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all rooted in that completely, sort of,

479
00:35:55,970 --> 00:35:58,531
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing that's beneath you, underneath you, you can't even conceive it.

480
00:35:58,891 --> 00:36:02,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can misconceive it, or not misconceive it, in which case you get it rightly.

481
00:36:04,293 --> 00:36:05,774
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the Mūla Vairāya Sutta point.

482
00:36:07,294 --> 00:36:08,895
[SPEAKER_01]: The attitude towards the form and so on.

483
00:36:09,495 --> 00:36:12,856
[SPEAKER_00]: Towards the matter and rūpa.

484
00:36:13,536 --> 00:36:22,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if there were no necessary condition of thing to be present, how could you even call about the arisen experience of a sight, basically?

485
00:36:22,060 --> 00:36:23,140
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha is asking them.

486
00:36:24,300 --> 00:36:28,403
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's asking in the most condensed, direct manner.

487
00:36:28,483 --> 00:36:30,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Or that's the manner it was recorded.

488
00:36:30,224 --> 00:36:33,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, the context is, do you see this is present?

489
00:36:34,147 --> 00:36:37,709
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, without anything to be present, can you talk about presence?

490
00:36:39,330 --> 00:36:42,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Without making that presence into this, that is present.

491
00:36:45,094 --> 00:36:46,075
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they say, of course not.

492
00:36:46,435 --> 00:36:49,817
[SPEAKER_01]: There needs to be a necessary condition for it.

493
00:36:50,297 --> 00:36:50,898
[SPEAKER_01]: A nutriment.

494
00:36:51,624 --> 00:36:51,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

495
00:36:51,844 --> 00:36:57,291
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, do you see with the cessation of that nutriment, what has come to be subject to cessation?

496
00:36:57,311 --> 00:36:57,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

497
00:36:57,491 --> 00:37:09,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while you're looking at these mountains, imagine suddenly, whatever, your optical core gets severed, I don't know, snaps inside for whatever, some bacteria ate it, and you just go blind.

498
00:37:10,960 --> 00:37:13,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be still able to have experience of that sight?

499
00:37:13,342 --> 00:37:13,682
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't.

500
00:37:14,042 --> 00:37:17,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, would you be able to stop that nutriment from disappearing?

501
00:37:17,065 --> 00:37:17,185
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

502
00:37:17,565 --> 00:37:19,206
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the definition of nutriment, by the way.

503
00:37:19,226 --> 00:37:21,088
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why there are those contemplations about nutriment.

504
00:37:21,528 --> 00:37:23,890
[SPEAKER_01]: They're... they're not in your control.

505
00:37:25,211 --> 00:37:26,252
[SPEAKER_01]: They're independent of you.

506
00:37:26,792 --> 00:37:35,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, nutriment is something you will eat or take, but you can only do so because it made itself available to be eaten or taken.

507
00:37:37,180 --> 00:37:38,742
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it's not rooted in you.

508
00:37:39,896 --> 00:37:46,222
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's even more profound than that, because, as I said, yeah, you can call it a nutriment, but in this case, even your own eyes are not yours.

509
00:37:46,502 --> 00:37:59,613
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they are part of the nutriment, part of the nutriment relationship, upon which the presence of sight depends, upon which your whole experience of visual objects and things and desire and lust and repulsion depends.

510
00:37:59,873 --> 00:38:01,174
[SPEAKER_00]: But my eyes... my eyes...

511
00:38:02,469 --> 00:38:06,990
[SPEAKER_00]: I've gone, but then I can still remember what I saw.

512
00:38:07,030 --> 00:38:07,890
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just remembering.

513
00:38:07,970 --> 00:38:09,691
[SPEAKER_01]: The absence is present now for you.

514
00:38:09,791 --> 00:38:12,811
[SPEAKER_01]: The absence of things used to be seen, but that is present.

515
00:38:13,511 --> 00:38:19,073
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you don't see presence in regard to that, the reason phenomena, you're still the victim of the same wrong view.

516
00:38:19,273 --> 00:38:28,855
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, what that's saying is that even if you lose your eyes, you can still become an arahant based on your previous experience, if you understand it correctly, right here, right now, in your thoughts.

517
00:38:33,907 --> 00:38:40,413
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, eh, Bhikkhu, does doubt arise when one is uncertain, thus, has this come to be?

518
00:38:40,873 --> 00:38:41,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this present?

519
00:38:42,014 --> 00:38:42,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

520
00:38:43,015 --> 00:38:43,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this present?

521
00:38:44,356 --> 00:38:46,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Does doubt arise?

522
00:38:46,038 --> 00:38:58,569
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're not sure about the present experience, is this, is this, is, is this thing, whatever that is, sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, is this thing present?

523
00:38:59,810 --> 00:39:00,871
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start questioning that,

524
00:39:03,300 --> 00:39:04,101
[SPEAKER_01]: You start doubting it.

525
00:39:05,442 --> 00:39:16,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if you start questioning, maybe you don't question the obvious presence of your sight, but you start questioning, yeah, but is it then that that form is necessary for the presence of that form to be?

526
00:39:16,469 --> 00:39:16,790
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

527
00:39:16,890 --> 00:39:20,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you doubt the... Because does doubt arise when one is uncertain?

528
00:39:20,292 --> 00:39:24,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Does it... does its presence occur with that as nutriment?

529
00:39:24,395 --> 00:39:25,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

530
00:39:25,496 --> 00:39:29,419
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whichever aspect you're not sure about, that's where doubt arises.

531
00:39:30,119 --> 00:39:31,120
[SPEAKER_01]: And see what the Buddha is pointing out.

532
00:39:31,140 --> 00:39:32,281
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you go beyond the doubt.

533
00:39:33,655 --> 00:39:39,236
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

534
00:39:39,696 --> 00:39:41,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, with this present, this is present.

535
00:39:43,517 --> 00:39:47,698
[SPEAKER_01]: But, this and this, or with this present, that is present.

536
00:39:48,579 --> 00:39:55,360
[SPEAKER_01]: But not that in terms of a second thing after, but more like a second thing that's also there.

537
00:39:55,440 --> 00:40:01,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence can be discerned in regard to that which is present, simultaneously with that presence.

538
00:40:02,368 --> 00:40:11,337
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start to think about presence separate from the object that is present, you made that presence into an object that is present, which means it's not the presence.

539
00:40:12,779 --> 00:40:14,381
[SPEAKER_00]: You're misconceiving the consciousness.

540
00:40:16,102 --> 00:40:16,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Another way around.

541
00:40:16,903 --> 00:40:25,112
[SPEAKER_01]: If you think you can access the form and matter and the world there without it being present for you beforehand, you assume

542
00:40:25,535 --> 00:40:29,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a direct relationship with matter and so on, which is, like, inconceivable.

543
00:40:30,216 --> 00:40:32,877
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way you can know matter is because it's manifested.

544
00:40:33,837 --> 00:40:34,397
[SPEAKER_01]: It's present.

545
00:40:34,997 --> 00:40:35,677
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Will said.

546
00:40:35,877 --> 00:40:41,638
[SPEAKER_01]: The matter finds the footing in your experience through you misconceiving its presence, basically.

547
00:40:41,839 --> 00:40:45,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Finds the footing in... finds the footing in consciousness, I think.

548
00:40:45,679 --> 00:40:46,359
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how he put it.

549
00:40:47,000 --> 00:40:52,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Finds... finds a footing means... Well, you make it to be that which it isn't.

550
00:40:52,421 --> 00:40:53,321
[SPEAKER_01]: You misconceive it.

551
00:40:55,161 --> 00:40:58,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Does it say it finds a footing in consciousness or finds a footing in...?

552
00:41:01,307 --> 00:41:01,727
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

553
00:41:01,807 --> 00:41:02,968
[SPEAKER_00]: It's supposed to be mājama.

554
00:41:05,090 --> 00:41:10,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, the four great elements, the four Mahābhūtas, are below your feet, so to speak.

555
00:41:10,175 --> 00:41:12,117
[SPEAKER_01]: They're outside of the scope of your experience.

556
00:41:12,858 --> 00:41:18,443
[SPEAKER_01]: You thinking you can relate to them one way or the other, they find a footing in your experience because of that.

557
00:41:18,903 --> 00:41:20,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of your misconceiving.

558
00:41:21,782 --> 00:41:28,352
[SPEAKER_01]: And, again, Mūla Pāryāya Sutta talks about uprooting these misconceivings and then, yeah, that the four great elements cease.

559
00:41:29,894 --> 00:41:33,579
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, the only way for them to exist were being misconceived.

560
00:41:36,323 --> 00:41:37,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if a person doubts,

561
00:41:39,562 --> 00:41:48,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether presence is independent of that which is present, or whether it's not independent, or whether it's both, whether it's neither, which one comes first?

562
00:41:48,844 --> 00:41:49,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, is this a cause for it?

563
00:41:50,364 --> 00:41:51,405
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not free from doubt.

564
00:41:51,945 --> 00:41:58,346
[SPEAKER_01]: If he clearly sees for himself, and this is nothing, this is as non-abstract as it can get.

565
00:41:59,086 --> 00:42:07,568
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's abstract, it's only because people have so much work to do, in terms of undoing their wrong views, so that then this starts to make sense.

566
00:42:08,871 --> 00:42:11,473
[SPEAKER_01]: But, that's not the reason to not start doing it.

567
00:42:14,736 --> 00:42:20,881
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is the most direct insight, basically, of paticca upādāna that defines the dhamma, that defines freedom from suffering and everything else.

568
00:42:21,201 --> 00:42:22,462
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

569
00:42:22,843 --> 00:42:25,305
[SPEAKER_01]: With this present, this is.

570
00:42:25,425 --> 00:42:27,306
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, the two simultaneous things.

571
00:42:28,367 --> 00:42:28,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

572
00:42:28,908 --> 00:42:30,929
[SPEAKER_01]: You can just say, no, it's only things that are present.

573
00:42:31,490 --> 00:42:35,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can say, no, all the things are present in my consciousness, or for my consciousness.

574
00:42:36,034 --> 00:42:37,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, you overshoot the mark.

575
00:42:38,527 --> 00:42:38,747
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

576
00:42:38,947 --> 00:42:40,668
[SPEAKER_01]: There is presence of things.

577
00:42:41,508 --> 00:42:46,150
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, there is the consciousness and I and form on the other side.

578
00:42:47,951 --> 00:42:50,752
[SPEAKER_01]: There is consciousness and ear and sounds on the other side.

579
00:42:51,413 --> 00:42:53,594
[SPEAKER_01]: And all the senses and all the thoughts and mind.

580
00:42:55,515 --> 00:43:01,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, presence is not the same as things that are present.

581
00:43:02,283 --> 00:43:06,124
[SPEAKER_01]: But presence, without things that are present, is utterly inconceivable.

582
00:43:06,904 --> 00:43:11,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're thinking it in your thoughts, carelessly, you are misconceiving it by doing so.

583
00:43:11,466 --> 00:43:12,746
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't mean, I must stop doing it.

584
00:43:12,786 --> 00:43:13,867
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't stop doing it.

585
00:43:14,287 --> 00:43:18,268
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you can stop doing is misconceiving while you're doing it.

586
00:43:18,748 --> 00:43:30,752
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, when you think about presence, you stop carrying that gratuitous assumption that it stands for that pure consciousness that is underneath, which is just a hollow, gratuitous, harmful view.

587
00:43:39,290 --> 00:43:47,658
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it continues, because it's doubt abandoned in one who sees as it actually is with proper wisdom, thus, this has come to be.

588
00:43:49,340 --> 00:44:00,730
[SPEAKER_00]: I say, yes, because it's doubt abandoned in one who sees as it actually is with proper wisdom, its originate, its presence occurs with that as nutriment.

589
00:44:01,651 --> 00:44:01,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

590
00:44:03,049 --> 00:44:19,859
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's basically fully, fully clearing any notion of yourself being involved there, being required there.

591
00:44:20,459 --> 00:44:22,240
[SPEAKER_01]: You see there is an experience of sight.

592
00:44:27,423 --> 00:44:32,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you see the necessary basis for that experience to arise, to manifest.

593
00:44:33,557 --> 00:44:36,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you see that that basis is utterly out of your control.

594
00:44:37,940 --> 00:44:39,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, inconceivable.

595
00:44:39,521 --> 00:44:49,827
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable that you could stop your eye from deteriorating, or your heart from having a heart attack, or your brain from ceasing to work, or your lungs from stopping to breathe.

596
00:44:49,887 --> 00:44:55,011
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable that you have a say over the form that's out there.

597
00:44:55,551 --> 00:44:59,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, can only be misconceived, basically, because there's no way of accessing it.

598
00:45:00,774 --> 00:45:03,056
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you fully understand that, you stop

599
00:45:03,628 --> 00:45:19,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, even the most gratuitous, the subtlest of assumptions you might have had about the conceit, vanity, sense of control, self-centeredness, self-view, all of that will cease because it just utterly becomes... that becomes then inconceivable.

600
00:45:21,437 --> 00:45:32,263
[SPEAKER_01]: All of your conceivings of self-view, I am, this is mine, this is for me, all of those conceivings are based on you misconceiving that relationship of that independence.

601
00:45:33,146 --> 00:45:38,909
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop misconceiving that relationship, everything else that you've been conceiving on top of it, becomes inconceivable.

602
00:45:39,690 --> 00:45:50,636
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha could categorically say, for somebody with a right view, it is inconceivable to take any determination as permanent, any determination as suffering, anything itself.

603
00:45:51,217 --> 00:45:54,579
[SPEAKER_01]: For somebody with right view, it's inconceivable to do this, to do this, to do this.

604
00:45:54,979 --> 00:45:57,640
[SPEAKER_01]: For somebody who is beyond sensuality, it's inconceivable to do this.

605
00:45:57,840 --> 00:46:02,103
[SPEAKER_01]: There are things that have become utterly inconceivable, right here and right now.

606
00:46:12,009 --> 00:46:13,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Slightly different, same topic.

607
00:46:13,650 --> 00:46:14,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, go on, go on.

608
00:46:14,450 --> 00:46:15,731
[SPEAKER_01]: I obviously wander off.

609
00:46:16,331 --> 00:46:20,274
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's keeping the same thing here, but he's saying, do you see this?

610
00:46:21,214 --> 00:46:24,876
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you could also say, do you see, do you see the foreground?

611
00:46:25,697 --> 00:46:25,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

612
00:46:26,997 --> 00:46:31,360
[SPEAKER_00]: And do you, do you understand that without the background, that foreground is impossible?

613
00:46:31,968 --> 00:46:33,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you understand?

614
00:46:33,168 --> 00:46:37,189
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be able to designate the foreground, unless there is a peripheral awareness of it.

615
00:46:37,490 --> 00:46:39,410
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not part of that designation.

616
00:46:39,650 --> 00:46:40,610
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm not...

617
00:46:41,251 --> 00:46:42,951
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I'm contemplating... That's exactly it.

618
00:46:43,151 --> 00:46:44,772
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

619
00:46:45,952 --> 00:46:49,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Without this, this cannot be.

620
00:46:49,653 --> 00:46:50,973
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a definition, but it's implied.

621
00:46:50,993 --> 00:46:53,354
[SPEAKER_00]: With foreground, background is.

622
00:46:53,414 --> 00:46:53,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

623
00:46:54,594 --> 00:46:57,175
[SPEAKER_01]: With foreground, background can be correctly discerned.

624
00:46:58,517 --> 00:47:01,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm not exactly thinking about consciousness.

625
00:47:01,179 --> 00:47:02,500
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not thinking about perception.

626
00:47:02,520 --> 00:47:02,780
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

627
00:47:03,341 --> 00:47:05,522
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha wouldn't even say there's consciousness.

628
00:47:05,563 --> 00:47:06,503
[SPEAKER_01]: He asked them this.

629
00:47:06,783 --> 00:47:07,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see this?

630
00:47:07,564 --> 00:47:10,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see a phenomenon of any kind?

631
00:47:10,526 --> 00:47:11,127
[SPEAKER_00]: A nutriment.

632
00:47:11,167 --> 00:47:19,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see the phenomenon arising with a nutriment, as in inseparable from its basis, inconceivable without it being present as well?

633
00:47:20,154 --> 00:47:21,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, we see that.

634
00:47:21,615 --> 00:47:24,918
[SPEAKER_01]: For the one who sees that, is he free from doubt about that?

635
00:47:25,238 --> 00:47:26,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, he's free from doubt.

636
00:47:28,686 --> 00:47:31,529
[SPEAKER_01]: For the one who sees that, would he be able to think?

637
00:47:32,149 --> 00:47:37,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but if the nutriment of this phenomenon disappears, phenomenon will still remain.

638
00:47:37,314 --> 00:47:37,474
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

639
00:47:37,674 --> 00:47:39,335
[SPEAKER_01]: That becomes inconceivable as well.

640
00:47:40,637 --> 00:47:43,319
[SPEAKER_00]: But for most people, they don't see the domain of nutriment.

641
00:47:43,479 --> 00:47:45,100
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's completely invisible.

642
00:47:45,521 --> 00:47:51,246
[SPEAKER_01]: No, because most people would see things... well, again, it comes from that

643
00:47:52,009 --> 00:47:59,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Original sati view of kind of assuming a certain external world to yourself, from which you observe things happening in the world to yourself, it doesn't matter.

644
00:47:59,933 --> 00:48:03,695
[SPEAKER_01]: There's this whole observation, meditation techniques, following your sensations and whatnot.

645
00:48:03,735 --> 00:48:07,357
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all about witnessing and looking at things, as if that's how you learn their nature.

646
00:48:08,198 --> 00:48:11,600
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all about foreground and collecting data about the foreground.

647
00:48:11,680 --> 00:48:17,823
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no discernment of the peripheral where the nature is, without making the peripheral another foreground.

648
00:48:21,398 --> 00:48:23,619
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you stop looking.

649
00:48:23,899 --> 00:48:26,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, my elbows, my knees, my breath, my tip of my nose.

650
00:48:26,600 --> 00:48:26,740
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

651
00:48:26,960 --> 00:48:28,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Body is there already.

652
00:48:28,741 --> 00:48:29,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Sight is there.

653
00:48:29,762 --> 00:48:30,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Sound is there.

654
00:48:30,722 --> 00:48:31,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Smell, taste, touches are there.

655
00:48:31,923 --> 00:48:32,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts are there.

656
00:48:33,864 --> 00:48:34,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Any.

657
00:48:34,204 --> 00:48:34,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Pick any.

658
00:48:35,004 --> 00:48:36,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can start discerning the principle rightly.

659
00:48:37,085 --> 00:48:48,211
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to pick any, you have to undo all these views that are kind of pulling you into that foreground even more, whereby it's about observation and witnessing and catching the moments of impermanence and whatnot.

660
00:48:50,277 --> 00:48:53,658
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when I look at this foreground...

661
00:48:56,479 --> 00:48:59,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, I know I have the information about the background, so... Sure.

662
00:49:03,021 --> 00:49:10,983
[SPEAKER_00]: This, this comes about through so many things which is not this.

663
00:49:13,756 --> 00:49:14,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Food, that's not here.

664
00:49:14,917 --> 00:49:15,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

665
00:49:16,577 --> 00:49:19,459
[SPEAKER_01]: And all of those things can be the right nutriment.

666
00:49:19,739 --> 00:49:28,442
[SPEAKER_01]: They will make you, they will enable you to sort of stop conceiving yourself as the owner, if they're seen within the arisen thing.

667
00:49:29,043 --> 00:49:30,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Not before or after it.

668
00:49:30,863 --> 00:49:36,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Not as a linear process, as one leads to two, leads to three, but one within one.

669
00:49:38,252 --> 00:49:39,233
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's exactly the point.

670
00:49:39,313 --> 00:49:39,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Take this.

671
00:49:40,054 --> 00:49:42,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Just say, my experience as a whole.

672
00:49:42,476 --> 00:49:45,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Me, sitting in this room, on a chair, holding a sutra.

673
00:49:45,878 --> 00:49:46,199
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

674
00:49:46,219 --> 00:49:47,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Just my birth.

675
00:49:47,460 --> 00:49:53,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Take anything and just have it present as a phenomenon that you just designated in your foreground as enduring there.

676
00:49:54,145 --> 00:50:06,135
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, through that endurance of that phenomenon, allow your mind to then think about necessity of food, necessity of a beating heart, necessity of a certain degree of health, necessity of freedom from accidents, necessity of freedom from

677
00:50:07,436 --> 00:50:12,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Too sudden, too large change of an elements, and then you realize, oh, that's why I'm alive.

678
00:50:13,140 --> 00:50:17,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because I am, not because I'm in control, but because the life still hasn't met, basically.

679
00:50:18,123 --> 00:50:20,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Those nutriments are still there.

680
00:50:20,584 --> 00:50:22,145
[SPEAKER_01]: And they're present, that's the whole point.

681
00:50:22,805 --> 00:50:36,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're not necessarily eating all the time, the fact that food that you ate, that you will be eating and so on, it's there, fueling this body, because of which all of this is present, your nutriment is present.

682
00:50:40,926 --> 00:50:41,406
[SPEAKER_01]: through that.

683
00:50:42,247 --> 00:50:44,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Your birth is present through that.

684
00:50:44,948 --> 00:50:46,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in the past, right there in the present.

685
00:50:46,689 --> 00:50:48,010
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that this body is here.

686
00:50:49,571 --> 00:50:52,473
[SPEAKER_01]: And I don't mean that momentarily, like you're born every second.

687
00:50:52,593 --> 00:50:52,773
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

688
00:50:53,994 --> 00:50:59,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Your birth was manifested and we call the manifestation of birth, we call it life.

689
00:50:59,737 --> 00:51:00,138
[SPEAKER_00]: It's life.

690
00:51:00,678 --> 00:51:01,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

691
00:51:02,159 --> 00:51:03,840
[SPEAKER_01]: So your life is what your birth is.

692
00:51:06,753 --> 00:51:15,558
[SPEAKER_00]: What about... so I can discern life, the general life, this is...

693
00:51:15,978 --> 00:51:16,598
[SPEAKER_01]: I am alive.

694
00:51:16,959 --> 00:51:22,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can say that the background to that is the not-death?

695
00:51:23,702 --> 00:51:24,462
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, yeah.

696
00:51:24,643 --> 00:51:28,705
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be like a very, very subtle, subtle...

697
00:51:29,532 --> 00:51:34,074
[SPEAKER_01]: present phenomenon, absence of death, that's where you are alive.

698
00:51:34,854 --> 00:51:46,699
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you want, in order to arrive at it correctly, not that you are not arriving correctly, but I'm saying for somebody who wants to try that, it might be more prudent to start with simpler, as in, it's still the same principle.

699
00:51:47,160 --> 00:51:50,761
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, oh, you start around and go, you know, go about.

700
00:51:50,821 --> 00:51:51,902
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's the same principle.

701
00:51:51,942 --> 00:51:54,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I am alive, right here, right now.

702
00:51:54,883 --> 00:51:59,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you think about it or not, whether you pay attention to it or not, your life endures.

703
00:52:00,087 --> 00:52:03,069
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, at any second, if I ask you, yes, of course I'm alive.

704
00:52:03,569 --> 00:52:06,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Your life is still there, enduring, as the utmost general thing.

705
00:52:07,471 --> 00:52:13,274
[SPEAKER_01]: It's even more general than your particular feelings or moods that might arise and last for days and so on.

706
00:52:13,294 --> 00:52:14,534
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm alive.

707
00:52:16,095 --> 00:52:25,160
[SPEAKER_01]: While you are mindful of that, and by mindful I mean, like, think I'm alive, and then allow it to go into that background, as in, stop trying to actively repeat to yourself, I'm alive, I'm alive, I'm alive.

708
00:52:25,220 --> 00:52:28,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Let it drift away, so to speak, without completely forgetting about it.

709
00:52:30,617 --> 00:52:31,057
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm alive.

710
00:52:32,658 --> 00:52:34,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I'm alive, I can talk here.

711
00:52:35,800 --> 00:52:37,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I'm alive, I'm sitting here.

712
00:52:38,381 --> 00:52:39,501
[SPEAKER_01]: I will go hungry later.

713
00:52:39,561 --> 00:52:40,562
[SPEAKER_01]: I will do this later.

714
00:52:40,942 --> 00:52:42,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I'm alive, I can read the suttas.

715
00:52:42,903 --> 00:52:44,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I'm alive, I can practice dhamma.

716
00:52:44,564 --> 00:52:45,464
[SPEAKER_01]: That's one direction.

717
00:52:45,944 --> 00:52:53,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can take that further and... or the opposite way and say, I am alive because there is the beating heart in me.

718
00:52:54,136 --> 00:52:56,098
[SPEAKER_01]: I am alive because my mother raised me.

719
00:52:56,158 --> 00:52:58,020
[SPEAKER_01]: My mother fed me when I couldn't.

720
00:52:58,541 --> 00:53:02,806
[SPEAKER_01]: I am alive because there is still food, edible, present for me as an option.

721
00:53:03,887 --> 00:53:07,170
[SPEAKER_01]: I am alive because the five aggregates are still not destroyed.

722
00:53:08,592 --> 00:53:08,992
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

723
00:53:09,913 --> 00:53:14,018
[SPEAKER_01]: But you need to... like, anybody can see that and anybody can say that, but

724
00:53:14,886 --> 00:53:16,247
[SPEAKER_01]: It's very hard to do it properly.

725
00:53:16,287 --> 00:53:18,528
[SPEAKER_01]: And by properly, I mean, see this paticca-saṃpāda.

726
00:53:18,929 --> 00:53:26,954
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why when Ānanda said to the Buddha, well, this paticca-saṃpāda, it's so obvious, of course, you see it in everything, because it's the principle of simultaneous presence of this because of this.

727
00:53:27,635 --> 00:53:28,915
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Buddha said, no, don't say that.

728
00:53:29,136 --> 00:53:31,157
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually a very, very subtle thing to see.

729
00:53:31,277 --> 00:53:34,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, of course, you've been looking at it for 20 years, so that's all you see.

730
00:53:35,360 --> 00:53:38,402
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people, because they don't see that obvious thing

731
00:53:42,440 --> 00:53:49,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Incorrectly means seeing that relationship of dependence, not just manufacturing it based on your ideal, but it's an upādāna.

732
00:53:49,723 --> 00:53:53,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while... say to yourself, I am alive, right here, right now.

733
00:53:54,364 --> 00:53:56,165
[SPEAKER_01]: I am still alive and still it's enduring.

734
00:53:56,185 --> 00:53:57,185
[SPEAKER_01]: My life is enduring.

735
00:53:57,645 --> 00:54:00,346
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't have to make any efforts, like, directly to keep my life.

736
00:54:00,386 --> 00:54:00,747
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm alive.

737
00:54:00,807 --> 00:54:01,507
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you're alive.

738
00:54:01,987 --> 00:54:06,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, why am I alive while I am alive?

739
00:54:06,769 --> 00:54:10,310
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I alive while I am alive?

740
00:54:12,379 --> 00:54:18,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're still mindful that you're alive, but you start looking for why within that mindfulness.

741
00:54:20,221 --> 00:54:20,841
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why?

742
00:54:21,481 --> 00:54:23,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is this heart still beating in me.

743
00:54:23,722 --> 00:54:28,943
[SPEAKER_01]: If that thing stops, I cannot conceive life anymore, no matter how much I might resist it.

744
00:54:29,763 --> 00:54:34,024
[SPEAKER_01]: If my pathways, airways, get sort of obstructed, my lungs collapse, I'm done.

745
00:54:35,364 --> 00:54:38,745
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is inconceivable to prevent, if it were to happen.

746
00:54:39,585 --> 00:54:42,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, I'm alive, right here, right now,

747
00:54:42,532 --> 00:54:45,032
[SPEAKER_01]: is fully dependent upon it, fully determined by it.

748
00:54:45,393 --> 00:54:46,993
[SPEAKER_01]: That is its core nutrient.

749
00:54:48,213 --> 00:54:50,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of operating organs.

750
00:54:50,954 --> 00:54:55,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Organs that are on the level of chair, table, everything else.

751
00:54:55,875 --> 00:55:01,076
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you just see them as a chair, table, and everything else, you're not seeing that nutriment relationship.

752
00:55:02,036 --> 00:55:08,117
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha never said, oh, yeah, look, this this stone is free from self, and it's impermanent, and you are... No.

753
00:55:09,053 --> 00:55:10,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're indifferent towards the stone.

754
00:55:11,213 --> 00:55:22,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Find things you're not indifferent about, and within them, discern their dependence on a certain nutriment, such as present body, living body, freedom from accident, all of these things.

755
00:55:23,716 --> 00:55:26,836
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha asked the monks in the citta, in the forest.

756
00:55:27,336 --> 00:55:36,518
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, monks, would you be upset if, like, like people come in this woodland where we are sitting now and start collecting all these twigs and branches and breaking them and burning them?

757
00:55:38,112 --> 00:55:39,813
[SPEAKER_01]: They said, no, of course, we wouldn't be upset.

758
00:55:39,893 --> 00:55:40,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

759
00:55:40,193 --> 00:55:43,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like, those branches are not ours, we don't care about them.

760
00:55:44,474 --> 00:55:50,636
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, so, you should develop the same attitude towards your own body, towards your own senses.

761
00:55:52,496 --> 00:55:56,557
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you need to see that it can't be yours, even if you want it to be yours.

762
00:55:57,138 --> 00:56:03,479
[SPEAKER_01]: And you see that it can't be yours, when recognizing the relationship of nutriment, the definition of patitsa and pada.

763
00:56:03,740 --> 00:56:05,020
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

764
00:56:07,278 --> 00:56:13,300
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no operating organs, or like heart, whichever we want to single out, all or one, doesn't matter.

765
00:56:14,080 --> 00:56:21,823
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no body being alive there, for which you have no say, you couldn't be present right here, right now, experiencing these things.

766
00:56:21,883 --> 00:56:27,385
[SPEAKER_01]: But that recognition must not be seen as before, or after, or one moment before, the next moment.

767
00:56:27,425 --> 00:56:27,605
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

768
00:56:28,025 --> 00:56:34,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Must be seen within the enduring peripheral awareness of the fact that you are alive while you're thinking that.

769
00:56:35,490 --> 00:56:38,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, yoniso, manasikāra, we spoke about before.

770
00:56:40,992 --> 00:56:50,094
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, in a way, it's like, all people would have to do, in order to see it, is stop trying to catch it, stop trying to assume it momentarily.

771
00:56:51,875 --> 00:56:58,597
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why in Ayurveda, we often talk about the time, as in people assume it linearly, and that's how you exclude yourself out.

772
00:56:59,157 --> 00:57:01,238
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is just allow things to be.

773
00:57:02,243 --> 00:57:04,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop trying to do it.

774
00:57:04,464 --> 00:57:05,724
[SPEAKER_01]: But not like, oh, just be.

775
00:57:06,345 --> 00:57:06,505
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

776
00:57:06,725 --> 00:57:09,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to discern something within that just be.

777
00:57:09,826 --> 00:57:12,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Because mindfulness in itself, well, it's not mindfulness.

778
00:57:12,947 --> 00:57:17,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Only mindfulness that's understood based on the principle of mindfulness can be called the right mindfulness.

779
00:57:19,209 --> 00:57:22,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean when we spoke before about the moods and different things.

780
00:57:22,491 --> 00:57:23,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Allow it to induce.

781
00:57:23,451 --> 00:57:26,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, as the Buddha said, monks, do you see this is?

782
00:57:28,204 --> 00:57:33,085
[SPEAKER_01]: This, that the Buddha refers to, can be pretty much any general thing you want to attend to.

783
00:57:33,125 --> 00:57:34,966
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just say to yourself, I am alive.

784
00:57:36,526 --> 00:57:38,187
[SPEAKER_01]: And don't try to force it, don't try to keep it.

785
00:57:38,287 --> 00:57:40,827
[SPEAKER_01]: You are still alive, even if you start doubting it.

786
00:57:41,288 --> 00:57:43,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but did I just say da-da-da all of that?

787
00:57:43,528 --> 00:57:44,709
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you're already alive.

788
00:57:45,149 --> 00:57:48,610
[SPEAKER_01]: And that already can be discerned in the peripheral of whatever you're attending.

789
00:57:49,450 --> 00:57:53,531
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once that comes down, then, within that peripheral of I am alive,

790
00:57:55,038 --> 00:57:58,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is a beating heart within this, I am alive.

791
00:57:59,084 --> 00:58:02,248
[SPEAKER_01]: I am alive because the senses are still operating within this.

792
00:58:02,668 --> 00:58:05,292
[SPEAKER_01]: I am alive because the nutriment is still available, of food.

793
00:58:07,195 --> 00:58:12,282
[SPEAKER_01]: I am alive because that random consciousness hasn't turned away from an object, as the Buddha would say.

794
00:58:18,915 --> 00:58:25,000
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm alive because death hasn't affected me yet.

795
00:58:25,520 --> 00:58:34,808
[SPEAKER_01]: But, see, the fact that death can affect that because of which you are alive, means you have no say in either life nor death.

796
00:58:35,448 --> 00:58:37,150
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you do is secondary to it.

797
00:58:37,790 --> 00:58:39,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Secondary as in not after,

798
00:58:40,891 --> 00:58:45,413
[SPEAKER_01]: In the sense of time, but secondary, it's sort of after, within it.

799
00:58:46,194 --> 00:58:47,814
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, it's not as fundamental.

800
00:58:52,997 --> 00:58:55,638
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you're... the only reason you're alive is because you're not dead.

801
00:58:59,440 --> 00:59:06,963
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why maraṇasati is like the quickest and the sharpest way to free yourself from any dispassion and lust and become an arahant.

802
00:59:07,784 --> 00:59:09,885
[SPEAKER_00]: The presence of the absence of death.

803
00:59:10,245 --> 00:59:10,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

804
00:59:14,693 --> 00:59:16,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's the... it's the condition for your life.

805
00:59:17,014 --> 00:59:17,915
[SPEAKER_01]: It determines your life.

806
00:59:19,036 --> 00:59:26,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if the absence of death becomes... sorry, if the absence of death ceases to be absent, your life's gone.

807
00:59:28,532 --> 00:59:42,380
[SPEAKER_01]: And see, you can discern that on that level of possibility, as in, you can discern it peripherally as a possibility, but it's a... you cannot possibly uproot it or do anything about it, which means it's certainty.

808
00:59:42,460 --> 00:59:43,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Possibilities are certain.

809
00:59:44,581 --> 00:59:48,303
[SPEAKER_01]: They're possible in their content, but certain in their nature.

810
00:59:51,165 --> 00:59:55,287
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's usually the problem, because people would dismiss

811
00:59:56,080 --> 01:00:01,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Possibilities, as if not applicable to me, usually because of the relative content.

812
01:00:01,425 --> 01:00:02,926
[SPEAKER_01]: It could be this, or it can be that.

813
01:00:03,706 --> 01:00:05,988
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact is, it will be one way or the other.

814
01:00:06,309 --> 01:00:20,461
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where that other, that other cryptic passage, it will, it will not be, and it will not be for me, means you stop being concerned with the content of what it will be, but you discern that whatever it will be, it won't be for me.

815
01:00:21,347 --> 01:00:22,929
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, I'm redundant.

816
01:00:23,289 --> 01:00:30,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, even in the utmost remote uncertainty of the content of the utmost remote possibilities, you are redundant.

817
01:00:30,515 --> 01:00:31,536
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what you discern.

818
01:00:31,877 --> 01:00:39,223
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's no... no amount of content will... can affect you, or reinduce the sense of self in conceiving and appropriation.

819
01:00:39,844 --> 01:00:43,087
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm trying to get to this point of nibbāna.

820
01:00:43,367 --> 01:00:43,868
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm trying to...

821
01:00:46,008 --> 01:00:47,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Where does nibbāna fit you?

822
01:00:47,209 --> 01:00:53,373
[SPEAKER_00]: And now that you brought that up, because that's one of the practices of discerning nibbāna.

823
01:00:53,413 --> 01:00:58,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I know we spoke about it before, and we got to discerning nibbāna.

824
01:00:58,977 --> 01:01:07,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you would discern nibbāna if the absence, sorry, the presence of the absence of your death is made present.

825
01:01:09,984 --> 01:01:10,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Because action is.

826
01:01:11,939 --> 01:01:12,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Death isn't absent.

827
01:01:13,560 --> 01:01:18,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Death is absent in its particular manifestation, but it's very present in its nature.

828
01:01:19,303 --> 01:01:32,349
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha said, if you practice mindfulness of death correctly, it's like, while you're breathing in, you're thinking, I might not live long to breathe out, because that's how present death is.

829
01:01:33,529 --> 01:01:34,150
[SPEAKER_00]: And I will not.

830
01:01:34,850 --> 01:01:35,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

831
01:01:35,470 --> 01:01:36,451
[SPEAKER_01]: If it happens, you will not.

832
01:01:37,551 --> 01:01:38,972
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually people are, I will die.

833
01:01:39,893 --> 01:01:40,934
[SPEAKER_01]: in 20-30 years.

834
01:01:41,394 --> 01:01:43,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, there were monks who would bring it closer.

835
01:01:43,135 --> 01:01:45,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you know, I might not finish it by the next morning.

836
01:01:45,837 --> 01:01:46,637
[SPEAKER_01]: I might not wake up.

837
01:01:47,017 --> 01:01:49,739
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, there are the ones who, I might die before I finish this meal.

838
01:01:50,940 --> 01:01:53,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, there is one, I might die before I take the next breath.

839
01:01:53,762 --> 01:01:57,804
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the one said, I might die when I inhale before I exhale.

840
01:01:57,844 --> 01:02:00,406
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, you are the only one practicing it correctly.

841
01:02:01,154 --> 01:02:04,677
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's how present that possibility of death is.

842
01:02:05,238 --> 01:02:12,664
[SPEAKER_01]: But because people don't see it, they only look at the particular as... particular poss... as in... the particular aspect of that possibility.

843
01:02:14,165 --> 01:02:18,649
[SPEAKER_01]: And by particular aspect, I mean the possible particular manifestation of it.

844
01:02:19,430 --> 01:02:23,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see that it doesn't matter the manifestation, the possibility is certain.

845
01:02:24,834 --> 01:02:26,035
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of it is certain.

846
01:02:26,156 --> 01:02:27,537
[SPEAKER_01]: So, death is certain.

847
01:02:28,979 --> 01:02:30,800
[SPEAKER_01]: If you understand that, that's what Nibbāna is.

848
01:02:31,221 --> 01:02:33,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that is the complete dispassion.

849
01:02:33,082 --> 01:02:35,143
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Ajahn Chah died before you die.

850
01:02:36,784 --> 01:02:44,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Fully, fully understand, develop your mind in regard to death, before it actually comes in a particular manner and swipes away your body.

851
01:02:45,870 --> 01:02:46,631
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Nibbāna is.

852
01:02:46,951 --> 01:02:50,373
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Purutusans will be terrified of Nibbāna, whenever they hear the Buddha describe it.

853
01:02:50,613 --> 01:02:51,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, they will kill me.

854
01:02:52,437 --> 01:02:54,417
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they would still be coming from the sense of self.

855
01:02:55,118 --> 01:03:06,260
[SPEAKER_01]: And assume the external point of view that it's in charge of everything, master of everything, controller of everything, owner of everything, yet this comes right from your very center of your being, from your heart.

856
01:03:07,020 --> 01:03:08,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's very frightening.

857
01:03:09,081 --> 01:03:11,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Because death...

858
01:03:11,101 --> 01:03:15,862
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, not dying, not... death as in end.

859
01:03:17,382 --> 01:03:20,263
[SPEAKER_00]: This is because

860
01:03:21,789 --> 01:03:22,690
[SPEAKER_00]: The end is not.

861
01:03:24,711 --> 01:03:25,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Say that again.

862
01:03:25,651 --> 01:03:29,473
[SPEAKER_00]: This is because the end is absent.

863
01:03:29,694 --> 01:03:29,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

864
01:03:30,034 --> 01:03:32,835
[SPEAKER_01]: The particular manifestation of that death.

865
01:03:34,116 --> 01:03:36,678
[SPEAKER_00]: But the end, the complete cessation.

866
01:03:36,938 --> 01:03:37,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Complete cessation.

867
01:03:40,080 --> 01:03:40,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, I see what you mean.

868
01:03:40,920 --> 01:03:41,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

869
01:03:41,280 --> 01:03:42,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what death is not.

870
01:03:43,001 --> 01:03:45,323
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what death isn't for a pūtugina.

871
01:03:46,863 --> 01:03:49,325
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, if pūtugina dies, it's not a complete cessation.

872
01:03:51,251 --> 01:03:52,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Death is another experience.

873
01:03:52,992 --> 01:03:57,553
[SPEAKER_01]: The complete cessation would be arahant and parinibbāna.

874
01:03:58,853 --> 01:03:59,993
[SPEAKER_00]: And that can't be present?

875
01:04:00,674 --> 01:04:02,094
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

876
01:04:02,494 --> 01:04:03,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's present for an arahant.

877
01:04:05,475 --> 01:04:07,435
[SPEAKER_01]: For an arahant, parinibbāna is present.

878
01:04:08,016 --> 01:04:12,357
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he has no problem enduring difficulty and discomfort.

879
01:04:12,737 --> 01:04:13,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Parinibbāna.

880
01:04:15,052 --> 01:04:17,373
[SPEAKER_01]: By developing nibbāna, parinibbāna is present.

881
01:04:17,453 --> 01:04:18,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, okay.

882
01:04:18,514 --> 01:04:20,355
[SPEAKER_00]: When the residue of the body breaks out.

883
01:04:21,196 --> 01:04:22,336
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, complete cessation.

884
01:04:22,356 --> 01:04:23,777
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the complete cessation, yeah.

885
01:04:24,097 --> 01:04:26,298
[SPEAKER_01]: And even trying to conceive what's after is inconceivable.

886
01:04:27,439 --> 01:04:39,446
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, people think, again, with the assumption of the external view, how we started this whole thing with that sutta, people assume the contents of your life, like, you know, eventually lead to death and then the new contents start, if you believe it, but if not, doesn't matter.

887
01:04:39,486 --> 01:04:41,187
[SPEAKER_01]: You still assume that linear progression.

888
01:04:41,767 --> 01:04:43,408
[SPEAKER_01]: But death is an experience in itself.

889
01:04:44,260 --> 01:04:44,820
[SPEAKER_01]: That has arisen.

890
01:04:44,920 --> 01:04:47,642
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have no idea how long it lasts, because you never experienced it.

891
01:04:50,983 --> 01:04:56,326
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the assumption that death just is about ending of these particular things, it's completely gratuitous as well.

892
01:04:58,547 --> 01:05:01,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But an assumption that death is the new beginning, it's equally gratuitous.

893
01:05:01,649 --> 01:05:02,629
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no idea what death is.

894
01:05:04,370 --> 01:05:07,232
[SPEAKER_01]: But thinking that it lasts momentarily, it's rather foolish.

895
01:05:08,335 --> 01:05:14,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the Buddha himself said, pains of death or those of dying, whenever he wanted to illustrate, could be the great pains.

896
01:05:16,421 --> 01:05:20,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know when they say, oh, the Sotāpanna can become an arahant at the moment of the death.

897
01:05:20,323 --> 01:05:21,323
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no moment of the death.

898
01:05:21,343 --> 01:05:22,704
[SPEAKER_01]: There's the experience of death.

899
01:05:22,744 --> 01:05:24,585
[SPEAKER_01]: And that experience can last for a long time.

900
01:05:25,205 --> 01:05:31,828
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, somebody with the right view will have plenty of time to become an arahant there and then, because there is nothing coming through his senses distracting him anymore.

901
01:05:32,408 --> 01:05:40,552
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why there's that sutta where it says, eh... Sotāpanna, if he doesn't become an arahant in this life, he becomes an arahant upon dying.

902
01:05:41,728 --> 01:05:47,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if he doesn't become an arahant in this life, or when he dies, he becomes an arahant when he's, like, in limbo, basically, in between.

903
01:05:48,014 --> 01:05:49,075
[SPEAKER_01]: During the dying.

904
01:05:49,095 --> 01:05:50,656
[SPEAKER_01]: During the dying, whatever.

905
01:05:51,357 --> 01:05:54,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if he doesn't become an arahant, then he becomes an arahant upon landing.

906
01:05:55,001 --> 01:05:57,463
[SPEAKER_01]: That is as close as you can have that translation.

907
01:05:57,984 --> 01:06:00,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if not upon landing, then upon the next life, and so on.

908
01:06:01,447 --> 01:06:03,069
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, people need to stop

909
01:06:03,596 --> 01:06:04,457
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an external assumption.

910
01:06:26,593 --> 01:06:28,734
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll continue, because he's almost finished his questionings.

911
01:06:28,754 --> 01:06:30,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Go on, yeah.

912
01:06:30,335 --> 01:06:31,376
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha continues.

913
01:06:31,836 --> 01:06:34,337
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, Because are you thus free from doubt here?

914
01:06:35,097 --> 01:06:35,878
[SPEAKER_00]: This has come to be.

915
01:06:36,098 --> 01:06:36,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, Venerable Sir.

916
01:06:37,099 --> 01:06:37,719
[SPEAKER_01]: This has arisen.

917
01:06:38,299 --> 01:06:39,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Because are you thus free from doubt here?

918
01:06:40,160 --> 01:06:42,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Its origination occurs without its nutriment.

919
01:06:42,421 --> 01:06:43,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, Venerable Sir.

920
01:06:43,222 --> 01:06:45,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Its presence is because its nutriment is present.

921
01:06:46,081 --> 01:06:51,344
[SPEAKER_00]: And Bhikkhus, are you thus free from doubt here, with the cessation of that nutriment, what has come to be?

922
01:06:51,524 --> 01:06:58,389
[SPEAKER_01]: If the absence of nutriment goes... sorry, if the... if the nutriment is absent, can the thing that's present because of that nutriment stay present?

923
01:06:58,749 --> 01:07:00,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely not, even for a second longer.

924
01:07:01,250 --> 01:07:01,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Beyond doubt.

925
01:07:01,771 --> 01:07:02,731
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

926
01:07:02,791 --> 01:07:03,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Without this, this isn't.

927
01:07:04,232 --> 01:07:04,632
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

928
01:07:05,253 --> 01:07:05,973
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's upādāna.

929
01:07:06,894 --> 01:07:13,518
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, Bhikkhus, has it been seen well by you, as it actually is, with proper wisdom thus, this has come to be?

930
01:07:14,762 --> 01:07:24,665
[SPEAKER_00]: And I will say, yes, venerable sir, has it been seen, has it been seen well by you as it actually is, proper wisdom, its origination occurs without its nutriment.

931
01:07:25,305 --> 01:07:26,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, venerable sir.

932
01:07:28,386 --> 01:07:33,067
[SPEAKER_00]: And he goes on, the bhikkhus, purified and bright as this view is,

933
01:07:34,225 --> 01:07:50,101
[SPEAKER_00]: If you adhere to it, cherish it, treasure it, and treat it as a possession, would you then understand that the dhamma has been taught as similar to a raft, being for the purpose of crossing over, not for the purpose of grasping.

934
01:07:51,522 --> 01:08:09,921
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no, Venerable Sir, because purified and bright as this view is, if you do not adhere to it, treasure it, cherish it and treat it as possession, would you then understand that the dhamma has been taught as similar to a raft, being for the purpose of crossing over, not for the purpose of grasping?

935
01:08:10,322 --> 01:08:11,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, Venerable Sir.

936
01:08:12,024 --> 01:08:12,224
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

937
01:08:14,624 --> 01:08:16,245
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't cherish the right view.

938
01:08:16,385 --> 01:08:19,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it means they were all at least sotāpannas, or even more.

939
01:08:19,526 --> 01:08:22,427
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't necessarily specify, because those specifications came later.

940
01:08:22,887 --> 01:08:24,548
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about seeing the dhamma or not seeing the dhamma.

941
01:08:25,728 --> 01:08:26,409
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no in-between.

942
01:08:28,029 --> 01:08:35,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they all seek the dhamma, they all have the clear view, but some of them might not be exposing their view.

943
01:08:36,770 --> 01:08:45,253
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, might not be dwelling on it, might not be drilling it, because it's such an amazing view, it's such an insight that they might already be happy with it.

944
01:08:46,093 --> 01:08:54,135
[SPEAKER_01]: But it wouldn't say... and then you kind of protect it in a way, automatically, simply by not trying to dismantle everything that needs to be dismantled on account of it.

945
01:08:56,276 --> 01:09:02,718
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he would urge people who've seen the dhamma, who've seen the nature of nibbāna, to make the effort further in that sense.

946
01:09:03,427 --> 01:09:03,487
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

947
01:09:03,507 --> 01:09:04,387
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just what you got.

948
01:09:04,808 --> 01:09:06,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't just cherish it and put it on a shelf.

949
01:09:07,169 --> 01:09:07,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Use it.

950
01:09:08,249 --> 01:09:09,550
[SPEAKER_00]: Use it and use it and use it.

951
01:09:10,050 --> 01:09:12,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it will only get stronger and then you'll go beyond it.

952
01:09:14,852 --> 01:09:15,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't be lazy.

953
01:09:15,612 --> 01:09:16,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't be distracted.

954
01:09:22,335 --> 01:09:23,936
[SPEAKER_00]: And the rest of the sutta continues.

955
01:09:24,016 --> 01:09:29,338
[SPEAKER_00]: A little bit about nutriment and then goes into pratītyasamvādāna, a more particular

956
01:09:31,020 --> 01:09:34,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course, because it's all about Pārtha.

957
01:09:35,942 --> 01:09:38,422
[SPEAKER_00]: I said it with birth, as condition.

958
01:09:38,582 --> 01:09:44,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Whichever phenomenon you take generally, you can discern its nutriment within it.

959
01:09:44,244 --> 01:09:47,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly why Pārtha starts in such a broad manner.

960
01:09:48,165 --> 01:09:48,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Not always.

961
01:09:49,105 --> 01:09:50,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Or ends, sorry, in a broad manner.

962
01:09:51,606 --> 01:09:52,286
[SPEAKER_01]: But not always.

963
01:09:55,687 --> 01:09:57,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes the Buddha would define it differently.

964
01:09:57,928 --> 01:10:00,789
[SPEAKER_01]: With not 12 factors, but 16 or 8 factors and whatnot.

965
01:10:14,728 --> 01:10:20,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhikkhus, remember this discourse of mine briefly as the deliverance and the destruction of craving.

966
01:10:21,072 --> 01:10:30,457
[SPEAKER_00]: But remember the Bhikkhu Sati, son of a fisherman, has caught up in a vast net of craving, in a trammel of craving.

967
01:10:30,537 --> 01:10:31,638
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know why they make trammels.

968
01:10:32,058 --> 01:10:32,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

969
01:10:33,579 --> 01:10:35,100
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's a lot more to the Sutta.

